From 46bc3ab31d147910709f4c94253add75ef5f2cb6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alex Perrin <44126061+AlexPerrin@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 12:15:25 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] feat: npc and loc op overrides for content that claims an op
 per player
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Problem. Content sometimes needs an npc or loc op for some players only,
ahead of that npc's or loc's own handler: until Doric's Quest is done,
Smith on Doric's anvils should bring Doric over instead of smithing,
while every other loc keeps its handler. A second handler on the same
key is not possible on the event bus, and moving the check into the
existing handler ties unrelated content together.

Change. Two small singletons in api/player:
- `LocOpOverrides.register(locType, op) { player, loc -> claims }` and
  `NpcOpOverrides.register(npcType, op) { player, npc -> claims }`.
- `LocInteractions` / `NpcInteractions` ask them first. When a claim
  matches and a `LocOverrideEvents.Op` / `NpcOverrideEvents.Op` handler
  is registered for that type and op, that event is published instead
  of the usual one. Otherwise nothing changes (no claims means one map
  lookup per interaction).
Both constructors keep working without the new argument (default
instance), so existing tests and call sites are unchanged.

Tests. No dedicated test; the first user, Doric's Quest, covers the loc
path in `DoricsQuestInteractionTest`. `assemble`, `test`, `integration`
and `spotlessCheck` pass on main with this change alone.

Data sources. None.

Caveats. The overrides are consulted on the op path only. If the
overridden npc or loc also has an Ap handler (`onApNpcN` / `onApLocN`),
that handler can still run first when the player is in approach range;
Doric's anvils use an op handler, so the first user is not affected. An
ap-side check (or `*OverrideEvents.Ap`) can follow when content needs it.

